{=============================================================================
  Lumped-capacitance cooling of a sphere in a fluid
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0343
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A solid sphere (radius 5 mm, density 3000 kg/m3, specific heat 1000 J/kg-K)
  initially at 400 °C is cooled by convection to a fluid medium at 20 °C
  (heat transfer coefficient 10 W/m2-K).  The energy balance of the lump,
      rho*V*c*dTdt = -h*A*(T-T_infinity),
  is integrated with the EES table-based Integral function; the independent
  variable Time comes from the EES parametric table (0 to 1000 s; the value of
  its last row is kept below).  The closed-form solution of the same equation,
  T_exact, is computed beside it.
  Inputs : r, rho, c, T_i, T_infinity, h
  Outputs: T, T_exact, dTdt
  Units  : SI - temperatures in °C, energies in J (the model uses no pressure)
  Source : S. A. Klein, F-Chart Software, EES example "Difeqn1"
           (MIT, via EES-Community/ees-examples) - see README.md
  Status : blocked by CS-GAP-INTEGRAL-2ARG (the two-argument table-based
           INTEGRAL is not supported); the runnable variant
           lumped_capacitance_cooling_coolsolve.eescode uses the
           equation-based INTEGRAL with explicit limits (see README.md)
=============================================================================}

"Physical properties"
r = 0.005 [m]                   "radius of the sphere"
A = 4*pi*r^2                    "area of the lump [m^2]"
V = 4/3*pi*r^3                  "volume of the lump [m^3]"

"Material properties"
rho = 3000 [kg/m3]              "density of the lump [kg/m3]"
c = 1000 [J/kg-K]               "specific heat of the lump [J/kg-K]"

"Constants"
T_infinity = 20 [C]            "temperature of the fluid medium [°C]"
T_i = 400 [C]                  "initial temperature of the lump [°C]"
h = 10 [W/m2-K]                "heat transfer coefficient [W/m2-K]"
Time = 1000 [s]                "time of the stored solution: last row of the EES parametric table (see README.md)"

"Energy balance to determine dTdt"
rho*V*c*dTdt = -h*A*(T-T_infinity)

"Integrate dTdt to find T as a function of time"
T = T_i + integral(dTdt,Time)

"Exact solution"
(T_exact-T_infinity)/(T_i-T_infinity) = exp(-h*A/(rho*c*V)*Time)
